ast  forever and so the dissolution of this  myth this covenant of who they were as  more Tukey's is also part of the story  it interesting the the commitment to the  south and particularly during the Civil  War and just commitment to slavery and  and to the south and the fact that  Alfred was really because he opted to to  resign from the US Army but chose not to  become a military figure in the  Confederacy I mean I just their  commitment to the South seems so much  stronger than I guess I expected them to  to be opposed to slavery or to be and  they were and I don't know I mean just  yeah I mean it's one of the things that  I think a lot of oh I'm sorry thank you  well one of the assumptions that some of  us bring to looking at a Jewish family  is liberalism because so many Jews have  played such an important and liberal  role in 20th century and even later 19th  century American life the the more  turkey family was liberal in many ways  actually but when it came to race they  were exactly in tune with their society  and I think it would be  rather unfair for us to expect anything  different than that as uncomfortable as  that fact is my forebears were living in  the same period they even met some of  the more turkeys at one point by  coincidence and I carry the same burden  this is this is our history yeah well I  am a historian and I started this  project and it took me about six or  seven years to read through the  manuscript documents because there were  simply so many so I had to cut off at  some point and I could have gone beyond  the Civil War I decided that that was a  good way to frame how this family had  formed itself and how the nation had  gone through a similar formative  crucible but there are many more turkeys  still among us and descendants not all  of whom carry that name and their their  name is not only on the historic house  in Raleigh but it's on fellowships at  Duke University because one of the  members of the family played a prominent  role in the university and you can also  read about them at a website about the  founding of Madera County California  where one of them went in the 1870s and  started a ranch which the family still  owns today after all these generations  question is whether Jacob and Caroline  ever reconciled this father-daughter  conflict that left them both so bruised  and and and hurt yes and no Jacob was  convinced by his other another of his  daughters Rachel to try to forgive  Caroline that that to hold you know hold  her in this sense  she felt she had been sort of exiled  really by the family and that even if  she stayed with other members of the  family she would sort of be turning them  against their father and poisoning their  those relationships so because that was  such a strong feeling of her as her  sister Rachel encouraged the father to  to you know relieve her somewhat and he  did write a letter to her in Tennessee  doing that and I think it made it helped  I don't think it completely succeeded  they did remain more in touch he wrote  to her he said please don't ever don't  ever bring this topic up again first of  all he said do write home do tell us of  your you know yourself and how things  are going for you whether your work is  successful and all that we do want to  know which I think was very reassuring  it to the degree that before that I  think she wasn't even sure he wanted to  hear about her at all and then later in  her life in the 1860s she ended up going  to Moby laughter Tennessee and and  teaching there and had quite an  independent and admirable life I think  really they're just very few women like  this who who'd struck out on their own  at of her class and she did come home to  Richmond at the time of the Civil War  and was brought back into the family  fold in a in a more full way though by  that time her father it was long long  dead another question  papers been preserved you know with  their particularly preserved in North  Carolina given all of the moves you know  to Richmond and everything else because  they weren't except for the boarding  house maybe they weren't a particularly  you know they didn't set up a major  tobacco companies or like some of the  other families right the question is how  are the papers preserved that that were  the basis for from my telling of this  story and it's a great question because  you know these things can seem so random  and I think they really are random to  certain degree we all should be  wondering how our lives and our eras  will be read and understood by  generations hence and I encourage you to  give that some thought because people  like me are totally dependent on people  like you but the more turkeys I have  references within their papers to them  reading over for instance there was a  family reunion in the early 1830s they  all got together in Richmond and they  brought out their old letters and they  sat around in the evenings and you know  people will be sewing someone would play  a piano for a while and they would sit  around the fire reminiscing about the  old days and for instance the days when  they ran the school in warrant and they  called themselves this happy band of  love and duty in those days and and and  so someone was keeping those had already  started keeping and collecting the  letters and from what I can tell that  the letters were passed down most of the  letters first of all were written by  women and the letters women were very  important in this family and so central  and I think they though some of those  women continued to hold them until - and  cast them down the generations to the  point in the 1940s there were people  going around North Carolina knocking on  doors asking people if they had any  papers in their attic and  was a famous guy who did this in North  Carolina with the wonderful name JG the  rule Hawk Hamilton and JG he thanked God  for JG he he he he roped him in so that  that's one collection but then there's  several more Alfred's papers are at the  Library of Congress for instance his  family kept all of his things and  they're in a beautiful collection there  so I was extraordinarily lucky and well  endowed with documents but I think it  part of it came from a sense within the  family that they were special and I feel  very grateful to them for having that  sense one more do you want it is in  trying to remember it is that the Hebrew  Union College and in Cincinnati in the  Jacob Rader Marcos archives and this is  this critique that Jacob wrote of the  books of the New Testament you know  which was a not a uncommon sort of angle  to take on these religious disputes  which was to say now look here and you  know Isaiah it says this but look in in  in you know the Matthew it says this  this doesn't fit together and I can't  remember the whether it's been  transcribed or not  Jacob's handwriting is extremely  difficult to read and so if it's  transcribed you could probably get a  nice photocopy from them if it's not I I  would say you better go to Cincinnati  documents and I guess you get used to it  but was that my I thought that's that  got a lot worse over these years thank  goodness for contact lenses or my  glasses would be like an inch thick but  but the women were taught very actually  almost  all the more turkeys had what was you  know considered then good handwriting  but but the women's was especially easy  to read it was more open but the thing  that got complicated was that they  filled their their papers so much yes  you can see how someone has turned this  on three sides and that's on the side  that the address was on so just around  the margins of the address cuz what you  would do is you have a big piece of  paper you fold it in half and then you  fold that and put the stamp on the  outside and so and then when times were  hard actually and they couldn't afford  paper especially during the Civil War  there are lots of letters in which they  and this is not uncommon at